<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Mathematics on Suyash's blog</title><link>https://theblixguy.github.io/personal-blog/pr-preview/pr-10/tags/mathematics/</link><description>Recent content in Mathematics on Suyash's blog</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-gb</language><copyright>© Suyash Srijan</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 05 Dec 2019 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://theblixguy.github.io/personal-blog/pr-preview/pr-10/tags/mathematics/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>allSatisfy</title><link>https://theblixguy.github.io/personal-blog/pr-preview/pr-10/posts/allsatisfy/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Dec 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://theblixguy.github.io/personal-blog/pr-preview/pr-10/posts/allsatisfy/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="header.jpeg" alt="Light pouring through a cave entrance, illuminating the darkness within"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A lot of programming languages provide a &lt;code&gt;contains&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;any&lt;/code&gt; method that is used to check whether there is at least one element in a sequence that satisfies a given predicate. For example:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#e2e4e5;background-color:#282a36;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;oddNumbers&lt;/span&gt; = [&lt;span style="color:#ff9f43"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style="color:#ff9f43"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style="color:#ff9f43"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style="color:#ff9f43"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;]&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;hasSingleOddNum&lt;/span&gt; = oddNumbers.&lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;contains&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style="color:#ff9f43"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span style="color:#78787e"&gt;//== .contains(where: { $0 == 5 })&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ff5c57"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;(hasSingleOddNum) &lt;span style="color:#78787e"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Now, suppose you wanted to check whether &lt;em&gt;every&lt;/em&gt; element of a sequence satisfies a given predicate. That&amp;rsquo;s pretty easy — you can invert both the condition and the result to achieve that. For example:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>